== Hyperlogarithm and differential Galois

$X = { x_0\,dots.h.c\,x_N }$, $N gt.eq 1$, $X^(*)$ is the free non-commutative monoid generated by $X$, $bb(C) chevron.l chevron.l X chevron.r chevron.r$ is the ring of formal power series with non-commutative variables in $X$. Consider $j : X arrow.r bb(C)$, $sigma_i = j\(x_i\)$, $Sigma = j\(X\)union { oo }$, $D = bb(C P)^1 - Sigma$ The differential equation #numbered-equation($frac(partial, partial z) F\(z\)= sum_(i = 0)^N frac(x_i, z - sigma_i) F\(z\)$)#label("10/31/2020-eq1") is of Fuchs type. Where $F in cal(O)\(D\)chevron.l chevron.l X chevron.r chevron.r$, write $F\(z\)= sum_(w in X^(*)) F_w\(z\)w$, $F_w\(z\)in cal(O)\(D\)$, then the differential equation becomes #numbered-equation($frac(partial, partial z) F_(x_k w)\(z\)= frac(F_w\(z\), z - sigma_k)$)#label("10/31/2020-eq2") Find half line $ell\(sigma_i\)$ starting at $sigma_i$ such that they don't intersect, and $U = D - union.big ell\(sigma_i\)$ is a simply connected, pick a branch for $log\(z - sigma_0\)$ on $D - ell\(sigma_0\)$. $hat(D) arrow.r^p D$ is a the universal cover, when there is no confusion, we shall still use $L_w\(z\)$

#statement("Theorem")[
Equation #ref(label("10/31/2020-eq1"), supplement: none) has a unique solution #display-equation($L\(z\)= f_0\(z\)e^(x_0 log\(z - sigma_0\))$) such that $f_0$ is holomorphic on $D - union.big_(k eq.not 0) ell\(sigma_k\)$ with $f_0\(sigma_0\)= 1$ (We also write $L\(z\)tilde.op\(z - sigma_0\)^(x_0)$ as $z arrow.r sigma_0$). And any other holomorphic solution can be uniquely written as $L\(z\)C$, where $C in bb(C) chevron.l chevron.l X chevron.r chevron.r$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
For $w in X^(*)$ not ending in $x_0$, write $w = x_0^(n_r - 1) x_(i_r) dots.h.c x_0^(n_1 - 1) x_(i_1)$, define #display-equation($L_w\(z\)=\(- 1\)^r sum_(1 lt.eq m_1 < dots.h.c < m_r) frac(1, m_1^(n_(i_1)) dots.h.c m_r^(n_(i_r))) (frac(z - sigma_0, sigma_(i_1) - sigma_0))^(m_1) dots.h.c (frac(z - sigma_0, sigma_(i_r) - sigma_0))^(m_r - m_(r - 1))$) in a neighborhood of $sigma_0$. And analytically continue this by #display-equation($L_(x_k w)\(z\)= integral_(sigma_0)^z frac(L_w\(t\), t - sigma_k) d t$) In particular #display-equation($L_(x_i^n)\(z\)= frac(1, n !) log^n (frac(z - sigma_i, sigma_0 - sigma_i))$)

#display-equation($L_(x_0^n)\(z\)= frac(1, n !) log^n (z - sigma_0)$) The $f_0\(z\)$ can be computed explicitly by comparing the coefficients in the series expansion that satisfies equation #ref(label("10/31/2020-eq1"), supplement: none). $L\(z\)$ is invertible since its leading coefficeint is 1, suppose $F\(Z\)$ is another solution, define $K\(z\)= L\(z\)^(- 1) F\(z\)$, then differentiate $F\(z\)= L\(z\)K\(z\)$ we have #display-equation($F\(z\)' = L\(z\)' K\(z\)+ L\(z\)K\(z\)' = sum_(i = 0)^N frac(x_i, z - sigma_i) L\(z\)K\(z\)+ L\(z\)K\(z\)' = F\(z\)' + L\(z\)K\(z\)'$) Thus $L\(z\)K\(z\)' = 0 arrow.r.double K\(z\)' = 0 arrow.r.double K\(z\)= C$

]
#aside("Remark")[
Heuristically, the solution might seem to be #display-equation($exp (x_0 log \( z - sigma_0 \) + sum_(i = 1)^N x_i log frac(z - sigma_i, sigma_0 - sigma_i))$) But this violates the non-commutativity of variables $x_i$

]
#statement("Example")[
In the case $sigma_0 = 0\,sigma_1 = 1$, $L_(x_0^m)\(z\)= frac(1, n !) log^n z$ and $L_(x_1^(n - 1) x_0)\(z\)= - "Li"_n\(z\)$, $L_(x_0^(n_1 - 1) x_1 dots.h.c x_0^(n_d - 1) x_1)\(z\)=\(- 1\)^(n_1 + dots.h.c + n_d - d) "Li"_(n_1\,dots.h.c\,n_d)\(z\)$

]
#statement("Corollary")[
By translation, there is a unique solution to equation #ref(label("10/31/2020-eq1"), supplement: none) #display-equation($L^(sigma_k)\(z\)= f_k\(z\)e^(x_k log\(z - sigma_k\))$) over $bb(C) - union.big_(i eq.not k) ell\(sigma_i\)$ with $f_k\(sigma_k\)= 1$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Consider $z' - sigma_0 = e^(i theta)\(z - sigma_k\)$, $L^(sigma_k)\(z\)= L\(z'\)$, then #display-equation($frac(partial, partial z) L^(sigma_k)\(z\)= sum_(i = 0)^N frac(x_i, z - sigma_i) L^(sigma_k)\(z\)$) Becomes #display-equation($frac(partial, partial z') L\(z'\) & = sum_(i eq.not k) frac(x_i, z' -\(sigma_i - e^(i theta)\(sigma_k - sigma_i\)\)) L\(z'\)+ frac(x_k, z' - sigma_0) L\(z'\)$) Hence #display-equation($L^(sigma_k)\(z\)= L\(z'\)= f_0\(z'\)e^(x_k log\(z' - sigma_0\)) = f_k\(z\)e^(x_k log\(z - sigma_k\))$)

]
#statement("Corollary")[

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Consider $z' - sigma_0 = z^(- 1)$, $L^oo\(z\)= L\(z'\)$, then #display-equation($frac(partial, partial z) L^oo\(z\)= sum_(i = 0)^N frac(x_i, z - sigma_i) L^oo\(z\)$) Becomes #display-equation($frac(partial, partial z') L\(z'\) & = - sum_(i = 0)^oo frac(x_i, \(z' - sigma_0\)-\(z' - sigma_0\)^2 sigma_i) L\(z'\)\
 & = frac(- x_oo, z - sigma_0) L\(z'\)+ sum_(i = 0)^oo frac(x_i, z -\(sigma_0 + sigma_i^(- 1)\)) L\(z'\)$) Here $x_oo = sum_(i = 0)^N x_i$. Hence #display-equation($L^(sigma_k)\(z\)= L\(z'\)= f_0\(z'\)e^(x_k log\(z' - sigma_0\)) = f_oo\(z\)e^(x_oo log z)$)

]
$bb(C) chevron.l X chevron.r$ is the free $bb(C)$ algebra generated by non commuting variables of $X$, there is a shuffle product $⧢ : bb(C) chevron.l X chevron.r times.o bb(C) chevron.l X chevron.r arrow.r bb(C) chevron.l X chevron.r$ defined inductively by #display-equation($e ⧢ w = w ⧢ e = w$) #display-equation($x w ⧢ y v = x\(w ⧢ y v\)+ y\(x w ⧢ v\)$) $bb(C) chevron.l X chevron.r arrow.r cal(O)\(D\)$, $w mapsto L_w\(z\)$ is then a homomorphism, i.e. $L_(w ⧢ v)\(z\)= L_w\(z\)L_v\(z\)$. Let #display-equation($cal(O)_Sigma = bb(C) [z \, frac(1, z - sigma_0) \, dots.h.c \, frac(1, z - sigma_N)]$) be the ring of regular functions on $D$. Let $L_Sigma$ be the free $cal(O)_Sigma$ module generated by $L_w\(z\)$. $L_Sigma$ is multiplicatively closed due to the shuffle relation, it is a differential algebra with derivation $frac(partial, partial z)$, and it is independent of the choice of the choice of branch of $log$

For $0 lt.eq k lt.eq N$, define truncate operator $partial_k : bb(C) chevron.l X chevron.r arrow.r bb(C) chevron.l X chevron.r$, $partial_k\(x_j w\)= delta_(j k) w$, it is easy to show that $partial_k$ is a derivation for shuffle product by induction #display-equation($partial_k\(x_i w ⧢ x_j v\) & = partial_k\(x_i\(w ⧢ x_j v\)+ x_j\(x_i w ⧢ v\)\)\
 & = delta_(i k) w ⧢ x_j v + delta_(j k) x_i w ⧢ v\
 & = partial_k\(x_i w\)⧢ x_j v + x_i w ⧢ partial_k\(x_j v\)$) The universal algebra of hyperlogarithm is the differential $cal(O)_Sigma$ algebra #display-equation($cal(H L)_Sigma = cal(O)_Sigma times.o_(bb(C)) bb(C) chevron.l X chevron.r$) With the induced shuffle product as multiplication and $partial = frac(partial, partial z) times.o 1 + sum_(k = 0)^N frac(1, z - sigma_k) times.o partial_k$ as derivation

It is well known that shuffle product is associative and commutative. Define Lyndon words $upright(bold(L y n))\(X\)subset.eq X^(*)$ by assuming lexicographical order $x_0 < dots.h.c < x_N$ and #display-equation($w = x_(i_1) dots.h.c x_(i_n) in upright(bold(L y n))\(X\)arrow.l.r.double x_(i_1) dots.h.c x_(i_r) lt.eq x_(i_(r + 1)) dots.h.c x_(i_n)\,forall r$)

#statement("Theorem", title: [Radford])[
$bb(C) chevron.l X chevron.r$ with shuffle product is the free polynomial algebra over $upright(bold(L y n))\(X\)$

]#label("10/31/2020-Radford's theorem")
For $w in X^(*)$, define weight $\|w\|$ to be the number of symbols in $w$. The induced filtration of $cal(H L)_Sigma$ is #display-equation($cal(H L)_Sigma^(\(m\)) = {sum_(\|w\|lt.eq m) f_w \( z \) times.o w mid(bar.v) f_w \( z \) in cal(O)_Sigma}$) Since $partial cal(H L)_Sigma^(\(m\)) subset.eq cal(H L)_Sigma^(\(m\))$, we can define $"gr"_m cal(H L)_Sigma$, by Theorem #ref(label("10/31/2020-Radford's theorem"), supplement: none), $"gr"_m cal(H L)_Sigma$ is generated by Lyndon words of weight $m$ which are polynomials in $N$ variables of degree $m$. For example, if $m = 1$, the Lyndon words are $x_i$'s, $0 lt.eq i lt.eq N$, if $m = 2$, the Lyndon words are $x_i x_j$, $0 lt.eq i < j lt.eq N$

$bb(C) chevron.l X chevron.r$ has two natural Hopf algebra structure. The first one has non-commutative multiplication given by concatenation, cocommutative coproduct uniquely given by that $x_i$ are primitive, i.e. $Delta\(x_i\)= x_i times.o 1 + 1 times.o x_i$, counit $epsilon.alt$ given the projection onto the coefficient of the trivial word $e$ and antipode a given by $w mapsto\(- 1\)^(\|w\|) tilde(w)$, $tilde(w)$ is the reversal of $w$. The second one has commutative multiplication given by the shuffle product, coproduct given by $Gamma\(w\)= sum_(u v = w) u times.o v$, same counit $epsilon.alt$ and antipode $w mapsto\(- 1\)^(\|w\|) w$. These two Hopf algebra structures are dual to each other

#statement("Corollary")[
The map $S : bb(C) chevron.l X chevron.r arrow.r bb(C)^times$ is a homomorphism iff $S = sum_(w in X^(*)) S\(w\)w in bb(C) chevron.l chevron.l X chevron.r chevron.r$ is group like, i.e. $Delta S = S times.o S$

]
#aside("Remark")[
$S$ may not be group like, but there exists $S^times$ such that $Delta S^times = S^times times.o S^times$ and $S^times\(w\)= S\(w\)$ for all $w in upright(bold(L y n))\(X\)$

]
#aside("Recall")[
If $\(A\,partial\)$ is a differential ring, $A$ is differentially simple if $A$ is a simple module over its ring of differential operators $A\[partial\]$, or equivalently that $A$ has no nontrivial differential ideal. A differential ideal $I subset.eq A$ is such that $partial I subset.eq I$. The ring of constants in $A$ is $ker partial$. An equivalent condition for differential simplicity is for any $theta in A$, there exists $D_theta in A\[partial\]$ such that $D_theta theta = 1$, otherwise $A\[partial\]theta$ would be such a nontrivial ideal

]
#statement("Theorem")[
Every differential $cal(O)_Sigma$ subalgebra of $cal(H L)_Sigma$ is differentially simple. The ring of constants of $cal(H L)_Sigma$ is $bb(C)$ and each element in $cal(H L)_Sigma$ has a primitive, i.e. the following sequence is exact #display-equation($0 arrow.r bb(C) arrow.r cal(H L)_Sigma arrow.r^partial cal(H L)_Sigma arrow.r 0$)

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[

]
#statement("Definition")[
The differential Galois group of extension $cal(H L)_Sigma\/cal(O)_Sigma$ is $"Gal"\(cal(H L)_Sigma\/cal(O)_Sigma\)= "Aut"_(cal(O)_Sigma\[partial\]) cal(H L)_Sigma$

]
#aside("Note")[
The differential Galois group is normally about the field of fractions of a differential ring, however this is fine here since $"Gal"\(cal(H L)_Sigma\/cal(O)_Sigma\)tilde.equiv "Gal"\("Frac" cal(H L)_Sigma\/bb(C)\(z\)\)$

]
#statement("Theorem")[
There is a canonical isomorphism of groups $"Gal"\(cal(H L)_Sigma\/cal(O)_Sigma\)tilde.equiv { S in bb(C) chevron.l chevron.l X chevron.r chevron.r\|Delta S = S times.o S }$, the right hand side has the group low of concatenation. There are also natural bijections between both groups and $"Hom"\(upright(bold(L y n))\(X\)\,bb(C)\)$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[

]
Both groups have natural filtrations induced from the filtration by weight. Let $U_n$ be the group of endomorphisms of $cal(H L)_Sigma^(\(n\))$ that commute with $partial$ and $⧢$, then $U_n tilde.equiv { S in bb(C) chevron.l chevron.l X chevron.r chevron.r\/X^(n + 1) bb(C) chevron.l chevron.l X chevron.r chevron.r\|Delta S = S times.o S }$. Since any group like $S$ satisfies $\(S - 1\)^(n + 1) in X^(n + 1) bb(C) chevron.l chevron.l X chevron.r chevron.r$, $U_n$ is a unipotent matrix group and #display-equation($"Gal"\(cal(H L)_Sigma\/cal(O)_Sigma\)= lim_arrow.l U_n$) is pro-unipotent. Its Lie algebra is isomorphic to the primitive elements ${ T in bb(C) chevron.l chevron.l X chevron.r chevron.r\|Delta T = T times.o 1 + 1 times.o T }$

#statement("Definition")[
A realization of hyperlogarithms is a differential $cal(O)_Sigma$ algebra $A$ and a nonzero homorphism $rho : cal(H P)_Sigma arrow.r A$, since $ker rho$ is a differential ideal, $rho$ is injective, and therefore ${ rho\(w\)\|w in X^(*) }$ form an $cal(O)_Sigma$ basis of the image of $rho$. The holomorphic realization of hyperlogarithms is #display-equation($cal(H P)_Sigma arrow.r L_Sigma\,w mapsto L_w\(z\)$)

]
#statement("Corollary")[
${ L_w\(z\)}$ form an $cal(O)_Sigma$ basis of $L_Sigma$, the only relations among $L_w\(z\)$ are the shuffle relations. Each element of $L_Sigma$ has a primitive in $L_Sigma$ which is unique up to a constant

]
#statement("Definition")[
Let $M$ be an $cal(O)_Sigma$ module of finite type, equipped with a connection $nabla$, i.e. a linear map $M arrow.r M$ such that $nabla\(f m\)= f' m + f nabla m$. Under some base $nabla = frac(partial, partial z) + P$, where $P$ is a matrix with entries in $cal(O)_Sigma$. Define the solutions of $M$ to be the smallest $cal(O)_Sigma$ submodule $"Sol"\(M\)$ such that closed under taking primitive, i.e. $nabla theta in "Sol"\(M\)arrow.r.double theta in "Sol"\(M\)$. $M$ is free over $cal(O)_Sigma$ since $cal(O)_Sigma$ is differentially simple

]
#statement("Lemma")[
The following are equivalent

+ There is a filtration#display-equation($0 = M_0 subset.neq M_1 subset.neq dots.h.c subset.neq M_n = M$)such that $nabla M_i subset.eq M_(i - 1)$ and $\(M_i\/M_(i - 1)\,nabla\)tilde.equiv\(cal(O)_Sigma\,partial\/partial z\)$

+ There is some basis with respect to which that $P$ is strictly upper triangular. We say $M$ is #emph[unipotent]

+ $M$ is spanned by its solutions, i.e. $M = "Sol"\(M\)$

]
#statement("Definition")[
Suppose $\(A\,partial\)$ is a finitely generated differential $cal(O)_Sigma$ algebra. $A$ is generated by $cal(O)_Sigma$ submodule of finite type $M subset.eq A$ with $partial M subset.eq M$. Then $partial$ defines a connection on $M$ which restricts to differentiation on $cal(O)_Sigma$, further we assume the ring of constants is $bb(C)$. We say $A$ is unipotent if $M$ is unipotent

]
#statement("Lemma")[
Let $\(R\,partial\)$ be a differentially simple extension of $cal(O)_Sigma$. $R\[y\]$ is an extension by adjoining an integral, i.e. $partial y = r in R$. If $partial x = r$ has no solution $x in R$, then $y$ is transcendental over $R$ and $R\[y\]$ is also differentially simple

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[

]
#statement("Theorem")[
The following are equivalent

+ $A$ is unipotent

+ There is an $cal(O)_Sigma$ basis of $M$ that can be annihilated by some $D in cal(O)_Sigma\[partial\]$ which is a product of $partial$ and $\(z - sigma_k\)$

+ $"Gal"\(A\/cal(O)_Sigma\)$ acts on $M$ and is unipotent, and $A^("Gal"\(A\/cal(O)_Sigma\)) = cal(O)_Sigma$

+ There is an embedding $A arrow.r.hook^i cal(H L)_Sigma$

In such case, $"Frac" A\/bb(C)\(z\)$ is an Picard-Vessiot extension, $"Gal"\(A\/cal(O)_Sigma\)tilde.equiv "Gal"\("Frac" A\/bb(C)\(z\)\)$ and $i$ in 4. is unique up to an element in this group

]
#statement("Corollary")[
$cal(H L)_Sigma$ is the unipotent closure of $cal(O)_Sigma$, i.e. $cal(H L)_Sigma = lim_arrow.l_A A$, where $A$ ranges over all finitely generated unipotent differential algebra extensions over $cal(O)_Sigma$

]
#statement("Definition")[
The regularized zeta series at $sigma_k$ is $Z^(sigma_k)\(X\)= L^(sigma_k)\(z\)^(- 1) L\(z\)in bb(C) chevron.l chevron.l X chevron.r chevron.r$ which is written as #display-equation($Z^(sigma_k)\(z\)= sum_(w in X^(*)) zeta^(sigma_k)\(w\)w$)

]
#statement("Lemma")[
#display-equation($Z^(sigma_k)\(z\)= lim_(z arrow.r sigma_k) e^(- x_k log\(z - sigma_k\)) L\(z\)$)

]
